{
  "theoretical_contribution": "This study introduces a novel theoretical mechanism linking user feedback loops to enhanced trust calibration and perceived transparency in interpretable large language models (LLMs), specifically within high-stakes financial decision-making contexts. It posits that increased user control mediates the relationship between model interpretability and trust, filling a gap in existing trust theories in AI.",
  "technical_contribution": "The research develops a unique user feedback loop integrated into an LLM, allowing investment analysts to adjust model outputs based on real-time interpretability metrics. This technical advancement enhances the model's adaptability and responsiveness to user needs, addressing limitations in current LLMs that lack such interactive features.",
  "empirical_contribution": "Through a six-month evaluation involving investment analysts, this study empirically assesses the impact of the user feedback loop on the accuracy of investment recommendations and user trust calibration. The findings will provide new insights into how enhanced interpretability and control influence decision-making outcomes in financial contexts.",
  "practical_deployment_contribution": "The study offers practical guidelines for deploying interpretable and controllable LLMs in high-stakes environments, specifically tailored for investment analysts. By demonstrating the effectiveness of integrating user feedback mechanisms, it provides a roadmap for ethical AI deployment in finance, ensuring that AI systems align with user intentions and enhance decision-making reliability."
}

